README.OSK -- vttest 1.7b on OS-9/68000ORIG/ is vttest as comp.sources.unix posted it, volume 7 issues 16 and 17(1986): Per Lindberg's VT100 compatibility test (Stockholm UniversityComputing Center, 1983-85), with Johan Widen's UNIX port and Rich Salz'ssplit into files, Makefile and manual page.The tests are unchanged.  vttest drives the terminal through sgtty --gtty(), stty() and ioctl(FIONREAD) -- in its UNIX arm, and OS-9 has noneof those.  Rather than change the twenty-odd calls in main.c, this treebrings its own:  sgtty.h, sgtty.c  (new)  struct sgttyb and the four flags vttest uses.      gtty() reads the terminal's options with _gs_opt() and reports echo;      stty() sets echo and, while vttest runs, clears the interrupt,      abort, end-of-file and pause characters -- a VT100's reports begin      with ESC, which OS-9's driver reads as end of file -- and in RAW mode      XON and XOFF too, so the keyboard test sees every key.  Handed the      struct its first gtty() filled, which is how vttest leaves, stty()      puts every option back as it was.  ioctl(FIONREAD) is _gs_rdy().  header.h  under OSK, SIGTERM is defined (OS-9's <signal.h> names none),      and the globals the header declares are extern except in main.c:      l68 has no common symbols, which the moderator's note in README      anticipated.  main.c    one line under OSK defines those globals before the header.signal() here only records a signal (unix.l), so ^C does not return tothe menu as it did under UNIX; with the interrupt character cleared itarrives as an ordinary key.MEASURED 2026-09-15 under os9exec: the menu draws, the cursor-movementtest draws its border and advances on RETURN, 0 leaves with "That's all,folks!", and afterwards the terminal's interrupt, abort, end-of-filecharacters and echo are as they were.  The tests are only as good as theterminal answering them; a screen emulator that is not a full VT100 drawssome pages wrong, which is the point of the program.